coercive control has been an offense in  the UK since 2015 but what is it is the  law working and how can you spot whether  it's happening to you  under the serious Crimes Act the  following must be true for someone to be  convicted of this offense  that the abuser is personally connected  to the victim their behavior has had a  serious effect on the victim and the  abuser knew or ought to have known that  their behavior would have a serious  effect on you your abuser's behavior is  considered to have a serious effect if  on at least two occasions the victim has  feared that violence will be used  against them or their felt serious alarm  or distress and it has had a substantial  effect on their usual day-to-day  activities before this law the only way  that domestic abusers were convicted was  if they were physically assaulting  victims but coercive controllers far  more subtle than that and more difficult  to spot especially as victims become  further detached from family and friends  the impact of coercive control varies  between victims but a really common  response is post-traumatic stress  disorder this can physically alter the  victim's brain and ability to function  the amygdala which triggers the body's  responses to stress is put on high alert  by the constant abuse and can actively  warp a person's ability to react  rationally and see things clearly  because of the nature of this Crime  Victims themselves might not even know  what's happening to them  here are the key signs that it's  happening to you or someone you know  isolating you from friends and family  monitoring your time spying on your  social media or devices taking control  over aspects of your everyday life such  as where you can go who you can see what  you can wear and when you can sleep  depriving you access to support services  such as Medical Services  repeatedly putting you down such as  saying you're worthless humiliating  degrading or dehumanizing you  controlling your finances making threats  or intimidating you depriving you of  basic needs  [Music]  mental abuse is the most difficult thing  to  explain to people I think that over the  years mine  was like a drip feed coming at me and it  came to a crescendo at the end Sally  Challen had been convicted of murdering  her husband who'd emotionally abused her  for many years she was released in 2019  in the first high-profile case of  coercive control being used as a defense  the court that convicted her wasn't able  to take into account the psychological  effect that being in that sort of  abusive relationship will have had on  her and on her mental state and the  state she was in when she killed her  partner  while her case showed just how powerful  an impact coercive control could have on  victims and how much that new defense  was needed the law hasn't transformed  the justice system in the way that some  campaigners would have wanted the number  of coercive control offenses recorded by  the police increased from  4246 in 12 months across 2016 and 2017  to 24  856 in 12 months across 2019 and 2020.  in 2019 just  1112 defendants were prosecuted for  these offenses a Government Review  acknowledged that the crime is likely to  be far more prevalent but there are  issues in both victims and police  recognizing the crime  I think what is necessary rather than  more legislation is better training for  police of recognizing when the situation  is dangerous and when an offense needs  to be charged  if you want to speak to someone about  the issues we've raised here are some  numbers you can call
 

